-- title:
--   Eisenstein torsion points lift to the 2-adic Tate module
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime. The assertion is: for all natural numbers $m$ and $M$ and every element $x_0$ of `JZero p` — the group of degree-zero divisor classes, modulo principal divisors, of the function field `modularFunctionFieldBar p` of level $p$ over an algebraic closure of $\mathbf{Q}$ — such that (i) $x_0$ lies in `eisensteinTorsionBar p 2 M`, i.e. $x_0$ is annihilated by the $M$-th power of the Eisenstein maximal ideal `eisensteinMaximalIdeal p 2` of the Hecke algebra `HeckeAlg` $= \mathrm{MvPolynomial}\ \mathrm{Nat.Primes}\ \mathbf{Z}$ (this ideal being the preimage under `eisensteinEval p` of the ideal generated by $2$), for the module structure `heckeModuleBar p`, and (ii) $2^m \cdot x_0 = 0$, there exists an element $x$ of [`TateModule 2 (JZero p)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15), that is a sequence $x : \mathbf{N} \to$ `JZero p` with $2^n \cdot x_n = 0$ and $2 \cdot x_{n+1} = x_n$ for all $n$, such that for every $n$ there is some $M'$ (depending on $n$) with $x_n \in$ `eisensteinTorsionBar p 2 M'`, and such that $x_m = x_0$.
--
--   This places a point of the Eisenstein $2$-power torsion of $J_0(p)$ as the $m$-th component of a compatible $2$-adic sequence all of whose components remain Eisenstein torsion, so that torsion statements can be transported into the $2$-adic Tate module. It is used in the construction of a generator of the Tate module satisfying the inertia-displacement and Eisenstein-annihilation conditions.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_tateModule_apply_eq_of_mem_eisensteinTorsionBar_two.lean

import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_JZeroNeronTorsionSheafV4
